<div class="head">
<div class="title">Closing the Romanized-Hindi Gap in Affect and Stress-Language Classification: A Controlled Multi-Seed Study of Pre-training Data and Multi-Task Sharing</div>
<div class="authors">Faizal,Prateek</div>
<div class="affil">GitHub: faizdevx &nbsp;|&nbsp; faisalariflko@gmail.com</div>
</div>
<div class="paper">

<div class="abstract"><i>Abstract</i>&mdash;Screening classifiers for sentiment, emotion, and stress-related language usually rest on one shared multilingual encoder, yet Romanized Hindi mixed with English (Hinglish) remains harder than English. We ask what explains that gap and which changes reduce it while keeping the evaluation honest. Using three public sources (Dreaddit for English stress language, English and Romanized-Hindi sentiment data, and BRIGHTER for English and Hindi emotion), we freeze the splits with hashed membership lists, remove duplicates across splits, and compare a TF-IDF baseline, a BiLSTM, and MuRIL over three seeds with paired bootstrap intervals. Replacing MuRIL by the Hinglish-pre-trained HingRoBERTa raises Hinglish sentiment macro-F1 from {{ m(STS,'sentiment','macro_f1','language:hi-Latn') }} to {{ m(HING,'sentiment','macro_f1','language:hi-Latn') }} ({{ dlt(STS,HING,'test','sentiment','macro_f1','language:hi-Latn') }}, 95% CI {{ ci(STS,HING,'test','sentiment','macro_f1','language:hi-Latn') }}) with no significant change on English, and an exact-match scan found none of the test texts of five or more words in its pre-training corpus. A dedicated stress model raises PR-AUC from {{ m(MT,'risk','pr_auc') }} to {{ m(STR,'risk','pr_auc') }}, whereas sharing one encoder across tasks helped none of them. Longer inputs gave no gain, and abstaining on the least confident 17% of stress posts raises macro-F1 from {{ m(STR,'risk','macro_f1') }} to {{ '%.3f' % sel[STR].risk.abstain_at_val80.test_macro_f1_kept }}. Integrated Gradients attributions are faithful for English stress but only weakly so for Hinglish. The stress model matches the original Dreaddit result, and emotion stays below published baselines. The system is a research tool for language signals and does not diagnose any condition.</div>
<div class="keywords">Index Terms&mdash;code-mixed text, Hinglish, sentiment analysis, stress detection, multi-task learning, calibration, selective prediction, reproducibility.</div>

<h2>I. Introduction</h2>
<p class="dropcap ni">Text classifiers for sentiment, emotion, and stress-related language are used to study how people write about their lives online. Most of them rest on a pre-trained multilingual encoder such as XLM-R {{ cite('conneau') }} or MuRIL {{ cite('muril') }}. For many Indian users, much of this text is Romanized Hindi mixed with English, usually called Hinglish. Code-mixed benchmarks such as GLUECoS {{ cite('khanuja_cs') }} and the SemEval-2020 sentiment task {{ cite('patwa') }} exist to measure how well models handle it, and the HingBERT study {{ cite('hingbert') }} reports that models pre-trained on Roman-script Hinglish tweets do better on code-mixed tasks.</p>
<p>Published comparisons are hard to interpret for three reasons. Fine-tuned transformers vary across random seeds by amounts comparable to many reported gains {{ cite('dodge','mosbach') }}, yet single-seed results are common. The English to Hinglish gap is usually estimated across datasets that differ in platform, annotators, and class balance, so language is confounded with everything else. Finally, a pre-trained checkpoint may have seen the test text, and shared multi-task models are rarely compared with single-task ones under the same budget. For stress-related language the cost of a wrong conclusion is higher: models of mental health built on social media data generalize poorly across datasets {{ cite('harrigian') }}, and the stress label used here records whether crowd workers judged a text to express stress, not a clinical state {{ cite('turcan') }}.</p>
<p>This paper studies one question: what explains the performance gap between English and Romanized-Hindi text classification, and which interventions reduce it while keeping the evaluation honest? We split it into four sub-questions. <i>RQ1:</i> does Hinglish-specific pre-training close part of the gap on held-out data? <i>RQ2:</i> does sharing one encoder across sentiment, emotion, and stress help or hurt each task? <i>RQ3:</i> do longer inputs help stress detection on long Reddit posts? <i>RQ4:</i> are the probabilities and token explanations of the resulting models trustworthy?</p>
<p>Our contributions are the following.</p>
<p style="text-indent:0;padding-left:0.2in">1) A reproducible protocol with frozen, hashed splits, duplicate removal across splits, validation-only model selection, three seeds, paired bootstrap intervals, and a pre-registered list of runs that may touch the test set.</p>
<p style="text-indent:0;padding-left:0.2in">2) Evidence that replacing MuRIL by HingRoBERTa raises Hinglish sentiment macro-F1 by {{ dlt(STS,HING,'test','sentiment','macro_f1','language:hi-Latn') }} on test without a significant English loss, together with a contamination scan of its pre-training corpus.</p>
<p style="text-indent:0;padding-left:0.2in">3) A controlled single-task versus multi-task ablation over three unrelated datasets: no task gains from sharing, and stress PR-AUC falls.</p>
<p style="text-indent:0;padding-left:0.2in">4) Negative and mixed findings: longer stress inputs do not help, and Integrated Gradients explanations are faithful for English stress but weak for Hinglish.</p>
<p style="text-indent:0;padding-left:0.2in">5) Data findings in public benchmarks: the Hindi tweet configuration is Romanized and partly English, one of its validation splits scores near chance for every model, and the English emotion data has no disgust labels.</p>
<p>The system is a research screening classifier for language signals. It does not diagnose depression, anxiety, or any other condition, and its stress output is not a clinical probability.</p>

<h2>II. Related Work</h2>
<h3>A. Stress and mental-health text</h3>
<p class="ni">Dreaddit {{ cite('turcan') }} provides Reddit post segments from stress-related communities, labeled for stress by crowd workers. Its authors report an F1 of 0.7980 for their best logistic regression and 0.8065 for fine-tuned BERT-base on the official test set (their Table 6). Chancellor and De Choudhury {{ cite('chancellor') }} review predictive techniques for mental health status on social media, Harrigian <i>et al.</i> {{ cite('harrigian') }} test whether such models generalize across datasets, and Benton <i>et al.</i> {{ cite('benton') }} give ethical protocols for this research. Those works motivate our restriction to language signals, our English-only validation claim, and our decision to keep all text out of the repository.</p>
<h3>B. Multilingual and code-mixed models</h3>
<p class="ni">XLM-R {{ cite('conneau') }} covers 100 languages. MuRIL {{ cite('muril') }} is trained only on Indian-language corpora, augmented with translated and transliterated document pairs. HingBERT, HingMBERT, and HingRoBERTa {{ cite('hingbert') }} are pre-trained on L3Cube-HingCorpus, 52.93 million Roman-script Hinglish sentences scraped from Twitter. On the Roman-script GLUECoS sentiment task they report F1 scores of 66.43 for HingRoBERTa, 61.57 for XLM-R, and 58.42 for multilingual BERT (their Table 4). Barbieri <i>et al.</i> {{ cite('barbieri') }} released a multilingual tweet sentiment benchmark, UMSAB, whose Hindi portion we use, and pre-trained XLM-Twitter on tweets. Dakshina {{ cite('roark') }} and Aksharantar {{ cite('madhani') }} document romanization variability and provide transliteration resources, which we leave to future work.</p>
<h3>C. Multi-task learning</h3>
<p class="ni">MT-DNN {{ cite('liu') }} reports gains from multi-task fine-tuning on language-understanding tasks. Pruksachatkun <i>et al.</i> {{ cite('pruk') }} study 110 intermediate-to-target task pairs and find that tasks requiring high-level inference tend to work best as intermediates. Gradient conflicts between tasks are one proposed mechanism for negative transfer {{ cite('yu') }}.</p>
<h3>D. Training variance, calibration, and explanations</h3>
<p class="ni">Dodge <i>et al.</i> {{ cite('dodge') }} and Mosbach <i>et al.</i> {{ cite('mosbach') }} show that seeds and optimization choices change fine-tuning results substantially. Sun <i>et al.</i> {{ cite('sun') }} find that keeping the first 128 and last 382 tokens of long documents works best on two long-text datasets. Guo <i>et al.</i> {{ cite('guo') }} recommend temperature scaling, Desai and Durrett {{ cite('desai') }} study the calibration of pre-trained transformers, and selective classification {{ cite('geifman') }} and maximum-probability baselines {{ cite('hendrycks') }} let a model abstain. Integrated Gradients {{ cite('sundararajan') }} is a common attribution method, ERASER {{ cite('deyoung') }} defines comprehensiveness and sufficiency measures, and Jacovi and Goldberg {{ cite('jacovi') }} argue that faithfulness should be evaluated explicitly. For significance testing in NLP we follow {{ cite('bergk') }} and {{ cite('dror') }}.</p>
<p>We did not find a study that compares Hinglish-specific pre-training, single-task and multi-task training, input length, calibration, and explanation faithfulness under one protocol. This paper does so on small public datasets.</p>

<h2>III. Data</h2>
<h3>A. Sources</h3>
<p class="ni">Table {{ tab_def('data') }} lists the data. Stress comes from Dreaddit {{ cite('turcan') }}, which has an official train and test split. Sentiment combines the English and "hindi" portions of UMSAB {{ cite('barbieri','umsab') }} with a community-built corpus of 3,190 manually labeled Hinglish YouTube comments from 23 videos {{ cite('ytdata') }}, whose annotator count and agreement are not documented. Emotion uses the English and Hindi (Devanagari) portions of BRIGHTER {{ cite('brighter','semeval25') }}, annotated for six binary emotions. The three tasks come from different populations, so we never merge labels, and all comparisons across tasks are descriptive.</p>
