<h3>B. Preprocessing and leakage control</h3>
<p class="ni">Text is normalized (NFKC, control characters removed) and URLs, e-mail addresses, phone numbers, and @mentions are replaced by placeholders before any model sees them. Exact duplicates are removed within each split. Rows in a lower-priority split that are exact or near duplicates (cosine similarity of at least 0.9 on punctuation-normalized character 4 to 5-gram TF-IDF vectors) of a higher-priority split are removed, with priority test, then validation, then train; test rows are otherwise untouched. Splits are grouped where metadata exists: Dreaddit validation is 15% of the training posts grouped by post identifier (train and test share no post), and the YouTube comments are split 70/15/15 by video, which gives only 23 groups. Because the official BRIGHTER development sets are small, emotion validation is the development set plus a seeded 12% of train. The final split membership is stored as text identifiers, split labels, and SHA-1 hashes of the cleaned text, with a SHA-256 manifest. Loading data fails if a processed table differs from the frozen list.</p>
<h3>C. Data findings</h3>
<p class="ni">Inspecting the files changed four assumptions. First, the UMSAB "hindi" configuration is written in Latin script: all validation and test tweets, and all but one training tweet, have at most 5% Devanagari letters. Our language identifier labels {{ lid.langid_distribution['en-Latn'] }} of its {{ lid.n_tweets_hindi_test }} test tweets as English. We therefore tag it Romanized Hindi and claim no Devanagari sentiment support. Second, the English BRIGHTER files have no disgust annotation at all, so disgust is treated as a missing label and masked in the loss and in every metric. Third, every BRIGHTER development and test text appears twice (as Track A and Track C copies with identical labels), which we collapsed, and ten corrupted rows were dropped. Fourth, on the UMSAB "hindi" validation split every model, including TF-IDF, scores near chance (about 0.35 macro-F1) although the same source reaches 0.55 to 0.62 on its test split. We could not determine the cause (label misalignment or a harder split) and therefore do not use that subset for model selection.</p>

<h2>IV. Method</h2>
