<h3>A. Models</h3>
<p class="ni"><i>TF-IDF baseline.</i> Word 1 to 2-gram and character 2 to 5-gram TF-IDF features (sublinear term frequency, at most 150,000 character features) feed one logistic regression per task. Emotion uses one binary regression per label and ignores rows where that label is missing. The regularization constant is chosen on validation from {0.5, 2, 8}. The model has 1.16 million parameters.</p>
<p><i>BiLSTM.</i> A word-level tokenizer (vocabulary of at most 30,000 types, minimum frequency 2) feeds 200-dimensional embeddings trained from scratch, one bidirectional LSTM layer {{ cite('hochreiter','schuster') }} with 192 units per direction, and masked mean and max pooling. It has 3.18 million parameters and no pre-trained weights.</p>
<p><i>Transformers.</i> MuRIL ({{ '%.2f' % 237.95 }} million parameters with heads) and HingRoBERTa (278.4 million) are fine-tuned with a shared representation. For a sequence of encoder outputs <i>e<sub>1</sub>, ..., e<sub>n</sub></i> we pool <i>h</i> = [mean(<i>e<sub>t</sub></i>); max(<i>e<sub>t</sub></i>)] over non-padding tokens, and compute <i>z</i> = Dropout(GELU(<i>W<sub>s</sub>h</i> + <i>b<sub>s</sub></i>)). A linear head per task maps <i>z</i> to logits: three classes for sentiment, six independent sigmoids for emotion, and two classes for stress. The multi-task model has all three heads and the single-task models have one, with identical initialization and hyperparameters otherwise.</p>
<p>The multi-task loss is</p>
<div class="eq"><div class="e">L = &lambda;<sub>sent</sub> L<sub>sent</sub> + &lambda;<sub>emo</sub> L<sub>emo</sub> + &lambda;<sub>stress</sub> L<sub>stress</sub></div><div class="n">(1)</div></div>
<p class="ni">with all &lambda; equal to 1. Sentiment and stress use cross-entropy. Emotion uses a masked binary cross-entropy</p>
<div class="eq"><div class="e">L<sub>emo</sub> = &Sigma;<sub>i,j</sub> m<sub>ij</sub> &ell;<sub>ij</sub> / &Sigma;<sub>i,j</sub> m<sub>ij</sub></div><div class="n">(2)</div></div>
<p class="ni">where <i>m<sub>ij</sub></i> is 1 if label <i>j</i> is annotated for example <i>i</i> and 0 otherwise, and positive terms carry the weight min(5, max(1, &radic;(<i>neg/pos</i>))). Class weights are used for emotion only: the training imbalance ratios for sentiment (1.41) and stress (1.10) are below the threshold of 1.5 that we set in advance. Batches contain one task only, and batches from all tasks are shuffled together each epoch.</p>
<h3>B. Training</h3>
<p class="ni">Table {{ tab_def('train') }} lists the settings. We use AdamW {{ cite('loshchilov') }}, PyTorch {{ cite('paszke') }}, the Transformers library {{ cite('wolf') }}, and scikit-learn {{ cite('pedregosa') }}. The best-validation weights are restored for evaluation. The same hyperparameters are used for all transformer runs, and the only tuning is the logistic-regression constant.</p>
