<h3>C. Calibration and selective prediction</h3>
<p class="ni">For each task we fit one temperature <i>T</i> on the validation negative log-likelihood so that probabilities are softmax(<i>z</i>/<i>T</i>) {{ cite('guo') }}; emotion uses a sigmoid temperature and per-label thresholds searched on a grid from 0.1 to 0.9 that maximize validation F1. Expected calibration error (ECE) uses 15 equal-width bins on the top-label confidence, and we also report the Brier score. For selective prediction, confidence is the maximum calibrated probability, the abstention cutoff is the 20th percentile of validation confidence (a target coverage of 80%), and the cutoff is then applied unchanged to the test set. We also report the area under the error-coverage curve (AURC) {{ cite('geifman') }}.</p>
<h3>D. Evaluation protocol</h3>
<p class="ni">Sentiment and emotion are scored by macro-F1 (emotion over annotated label cells only), and stress by F1 of the positive class, PR-AUC, ROC-AUC, and ECE. Sentiment is reported separately for English tweets, Romanized-Hindi tweets, and Hinglish YouTube comments. All selection uses validation data. Only the runs named in the plan touch the test set: the multi-task MuRIL model, the three single-task MuRIL models, and HingRoBERTa, which was evaluated once after it won on validation. The 256-token stress variants were compared on validation only. System pairs are compared by a paired bootstrap {{ cite('bergk','dror') }}: we resample test examples 1,000 times, average each metric over the three seeds on the same resample for both systems, and report the 2.5th and 97.5th percentiles of the difference. This interval covers test-sample noise only, so seed standard deviations are reported separately.</p>
<h3>E. Faithfulness test</h3>
<p class="ni">The application serves Integrated Gradients {{ cite('sundararajan') }} attributions over the input embeddings (16 steps, zero baseline). We test them with the comprehensiveness measure of ERASER {{ cite('deyoung') }}: for 60 validation examples per group, we delete the <i>k</i> = &lceil;0.2<i>n</i>&rceil; content tokens with the highest attribution, a random set of <i>k</i> tokens (mean of five draws), and the <i>k</i> lowest-attribution tokens, and measure the drop in the probability of the predicted class. Faithful attributions should give a larger drop for the top set than for the random set {{ cite('jacovi') }}.</p>
<h3>F. Reproducibility engineering</h3>
<p class="ni">Each run stores its resolved configuration, package versions, git commit, per-epoch losses, calibrated per-example probabilities (without text), and timing. Training writes an atomic snapshot of weights, optimizer, scheduler, random state, and position within the epoch every ten minutes, so a machine restart costs at most that long and repeats no batch. A unit test covers interruption and resumption. Seeding happens before the model is built: an early version initialized the task heads before seeding, so "seed 13" differed between processes, and the regression is now tested. Our multi-task seed-13 run reproduces the earlier October run exactly at every epoch.</p>

<h2>V. Experiments and Results</h2>
<h3>A. Setup and deviations</h3>
<p class="ni">No GPU was available, so every run used four CPU cores in single precision, roughly one day of compute in total for 21 runs. We therefore used three seeds instead of five and trained single-task stress models, which cost about a quarter of a multi-task run. A learning-rate grid, an XLM-R control, and normalization and transliteration experiments were not run, and the manual error taxonomy has not been annotated.</p>
<h3>B. Baselines and the multi-task transformer</h3>
<p class="ni">{{ tref('orig') }} compares the three model families from the first single-seed run. The BiLSTM does not beat TF-IDF: its paired differences from the baseline on test are {{ '%+.3f' % bd.bilstm.sentiment_macro_f1.mean }} macro-F1 for sentiment (95% CI [{{ '%+.3f' % bd.bilstm.sentiment_macro_f1.lo }}, {{ '%+.3f' % bd.bilstm.sentiment_macro_f1.hi }}]) and {{ '%+.3f' % bd.bilstm.risk_f1.mean }} F1 for stress (CI [{{ '%+.3f' % bd.bilstm.risk_f1.lo }}, {{ '%+.3f' % bd.bilstm.risk_f1.hi }}]), and its validation F1 plateaus after about six epochs while training loss keeps falling. MuRIL gains {{ '%+.3f' % bd.transformer.sentiment_macro_f1.mean }} on sentiment (CI [{{ '%+.3f' % bd.transformer.sentiment_macro_f1.lo }}, {{ '%+.3f' % bd.transformer.sentiment_macro_f1.hi }}]) and {{ '%+.3f' % bd.transformer.risk_f1.mean }} on stress F1 (CI [{{ '%+.3f' % bd.transformer.risk_f1.lo }}, {{ '%+.3f' % bd.transformer.risk_f1.hi }}]). Its stress PR-AUC is not higher than that of TF-IDF ({{ '%+.3f' % bd.transformer.risk_pr_auc.mean }}, CI [{{ '%+.3f' % bd.transformer.risk_pr_auc.lo }}, {{ '%+.3f' % bd.transformer.risk_pr_auc.hi }}]), so its F1 advantage comes from the operating point and not from better ranking. It also needs 89 ms per request on CPU against 4 ms.</p>
