<p>The confusion pattern explains where the gain comes from. Pooling the three seeds' probabilities, MuRIL predicts negative for {{ errb.C_ST_sentiment_muril.confusion_seed_avg_hiLatn.neg.pos }} positive and {{ errb.C_ST_sentiment_muril.confusion_seed_avg_hiLatn.neg.neu }} neutral Hinglish test comments, and HingRoBERTa does so for {{ errb.C_ST_sentiment_hingroberta.confusion_seed_avg_hiLatn.neg.pos }} and {{ errb.C_ST_sentiment_hingroberta.confusion_seed_avg_hiLatn.neg.neu }}. Negative recall is about unchanged. Inside the Romanized-Hindi tweets, which share a platform and annotation pipeline, tweets that our identifier tags as English score {{ '%.3f' % lid.by_model.C_ST_sentiment_muril['en-Latn'].macro_f1_mean }} (MuRIL) and {{ '%.3f' % lid.by_model.C_ST_sentiment_hingroberta['en-Latn'].macro_f1_mean }} (HingRoBERTa), and those tagged Romanized Hindi score {{ '%.3f' % lid.by_model.C_ST_sentiment_muril['hi-Latn'].macro_f1_mean }} and {{ '%.3f' % lid.by_model.C_ST_sentiment_hingroberta['hi-Latn'].macro_f1_mean }}. A gap of about 0.1 therefore remains within one source. The identifier is itself a classifier (recall 0.92 on Hinglish YouTube text and 0.99 on English), so this split is indicative.</p>
<p><i>Contamination.</i> HingRoBERTa was pre-trained on tweets, so we scanned all {{ '{:,}'.format(overlap.corpus_lines_scanned) }} lines of a public re-upload of the corpus for exact matches (after normalization) with Hinglish validation and test texts of at least five words. None of the {{ '{:,}'.format(overlap.checked_by_split_source['test|tweets-hindi'] + overlap.checked_by_split_source['test|youtube-hinglish']) }} test texts matched, and one of {{ '{:,}'.format(overlap.checked_by_split_source['val|tweets-hindi'] + overlap.checked_by_split_source['val|youtube-hinglish']) }} validation texts did. Near duplicates were not checked, and the re-upload is not verified identical to the original release.</p>

<h3>D. Stress detection and multi-task sharing (RQ2)</h3>
<p class="ni">{{ tref('main') }} gives the three-seed test results and {{ tref('mtst') }} the controlled comparison. A dedicated stress model has higher PR-AUC than the multi-task model ({{ m(STR,'risk','pr_auc') }} against {{ m(MT,'risk','pr_auc') }}). The multi-task minus single-task difference is {{ dci(STR,MT,'test','risk','pr_auc') }}, and the F1 difference is {{ dci(STR,MT,'test','risk','f1') }}, so only ranking quality changes. Calibration moves the other way: ECE is {{ m(STR,'risk','ece') }} for the single-task model and {{ m(MT,'risk','ece') }} for the multi-task one. For sentiment and emotion, sharing neither helps nor hurts within our resolution. The three datasets differ in population, platform, and annotators, and some tasks transfer much better than others {{ cite('pruk') }}, so a positive effect was not guaranteed; the gains reported for related tasks {{ cite('liu') }} may not extend to this setting. We did not test gradient-conflict remedies {{ cite('yu') }}, which the stress result would justify.</p>
