<h3>F. Probabilities and abstention (RQ4)</h3>
<p class="ni">After temperature scaling, test ECE is {{ m(STR,'risk','ece') }} for single-task stress, {{ m(MT,'risk','ece') }} for the multi-task stress head, {{ m(STS,'sentiment','ece') }} for MuRIL sentiment, and {{ m(HING,'sentiment','ece') }} for HingRoBERTa sentiment. HingRoBERTa has the lower Brier score ({{ m(HING,'sentiment','brier') }} against {{ m(STS,'sentiment','brier') }}) and the higher ECE, so calibration and discrimination should be reported separately. Abstention helps every model (Fig. {{ fig_def('rc') }}). With the cutoff fixed on validation, the stress model answers {{ '%.0f' % (100*sel[STR].risk.abstain_at_val80.test_coverage) }}% of test posts at macro-F1 {{ '%.3f' % sel[STR].risk.abstain_at_val80.test_macro_f1_kept }} (against {{ '%.3f' % sel[STR].risk.abstain_at_val80.test_macro_f1_all }} on all posts), MuRIL sentiment answers {{ '%.0f' % (100*sel[STS].sentiment.abstain_at_val80.test_coverage) }}% at {{ '%.3f' % sel[STS].sentiment.abstain_at_val80.test_macro_f1_kept }} (against {{ '%.3f' % sel[STS].sentiment.abstain_at_val80.test_macro_f1_all }}), and HingRoBERTa answers {{ '%.0f' % (100*sel[HING].sentiment.abstain_at_val80.test_coverage) }}% at {{ '%.3f' % sel[HING].sentiment.abstain_at_val80.test_macro_f1_kept }} (against {{ '%.3f' % sel[HING].sentiment.abstain_at_val80.test_macro_f1_all }}). Coverage on test differs from the 80% target because the confidence distribution shifts between splits. A low-confidence output means the model is uncertain about the text. It carries no information about the author.</p>
