<h3>G. Explanation faithfulness</h3>
<p class="ni">{{ fref('faith') }} shows the deletion test. For English stress, deleting the top-attributed tokens lowers the predicted-class probability by {{ fth('risk:en-Latn','mean_drop_top_k') }} against {{ fth('risk:en-Latn','mean_drop_random_k') }} for random tokens (difference {{ '%+.3f' % faith['risk:en-Latn'].top_minus_random }}, bootstrap CI [{{ '%+.3f' % faith['risk:en-Latn'].ci95[0] }}, {{ '%+.3f' % faith['risk:en-Latn'].ci95[1] }}], top set larger in {{ '%.0f' % (100*faith['risk:en-Latn'].share_examples_top_gt_random) }}% of examples). English sentiment gives {{ '%+.3f' % faith['sentiment:en-Latn'].top_minus_random }} (CI [{{ '%+.3f' % faith['sentiment:en-Latn'].ci95[0] }}, {{ '%+.3f' % faith['sentiment:en-Latn'].ci95[1] }}], {{ '%.0f' % (100*faith['sentiment:en-Latn'].share_examples_top_gt_random) }}% of examples). Hinglish sentiment gives only {{ '%+.3f' % faith['sentiment:hi-Latn'].top_minus_random }} (CI [{{ '%+.3f' % faith['sentiment:hi-Latn'].ci95[0] }}, {{ '%+.3f' % faith['sentiment:hi-Latn'].ci95[1] }}], {{ '%.0f' % (100*faith['sentiment:hi-Latn'].share_examples_top_gt_random) }}% of examples), and for {{ '%.0f' % (100 - 100*faith['sentiment:hi-Latn'].share_examples_top_gt_random) }}% of Hinglish examples the highlighted tokens matter no more than random ones. We measured comprehensiveness only, on 60 examples per group and one model, so these figures are an initial check and not a full audit. The application now states this limit next to every explanation.</p>
<div class="fig"><img src="figures/fig_faithfulness.png" alt="Deletion test"><div class="cap">Fig. {{ fig_def('faith') }}. Drop in predicted-class probability after deleting the top-k, random-k, or bottom-k tokens (k is 20% of content tokens; 60 validation examples per group).</div></div>

<h3>H. Error analysis</h3>
<p class="ni">On the Hinglish test set the error rate is {{ '%.3f' % errb.C_ST_sentiment_muril.by_language['hi-Latn'].error_rate }} for MuRIL and {{ '%.3f' % errb.C_ST_sentiment_hingroberta.by_language['hi-Latn'].error_rate }} for HingRoBERTa, against {{ '%.3f' % errb.C_ST_sentiment_muril.by_language['en-Latn'].error_rate }} and {{ '%.3f' % errb.C_ST_sentiment_hingroberta.by_language['en-Latn'].error_rate }} on English. Texts under eight words are harder ({{ '%.3f' % errb.C_ST_sentiment_muril.by_length['short(<8)'].error_rate }} and {{ '%.3f' % errb.C_ST_sentiment_hingroberta.by_length['short(<8)'].error_rate }}) than texts of 8 to 39 words ({{ '%.3f' % errb.C_ST_sentiment_muril.by_length['medium(8-39)'].error_rate }} and {{ '%.3f' % errb.C_ST_sentiment_hingroberta.by_length['medium(8-39)'].error_rate }}). Nearly all Dreaddit test posts are long (693 of 715 have 40 or more words), so the short-text behavior of the stress model is untested. The tweet language-identification split above and the near-chance validation subset (Section III-C) are the two data effects we can quantify. Categories such as spelling variation, code switching, and sarcasm need human annotation; we exported a sheet of 200 validation errors for that purpose but have not annotated it, so we report no category counts.</p>

