<p>On Dreaddit our stress model matches the original paper: F1 {{ m(STR,'risk','f1') }} against 0.7980 for their best logistic regression and 0.8065 for BERT-base, on the same official test posts. The size of the Hinglish gain (+{{ '%.1f' % (100*dnum(STS,HING,'test','sentiment','macro_f1','language:hi-Latn')) }} macro-F1 points over MuRIL) is similar to the +4.9 F1 points that {{ cite('hingbert') }} report over XLM-R on a different benchmark, which supports the claim that Hinglish pre-training helps code-mixed sentiment. On the shared UMSAB Hindi test file HingRoBERTa scores about five points above the published XLM-Twitter figure, but the training data differs, so we treat that as an indication and not as a result. Our emotion models are clearly below the published baselines on both languages. We trained them for three epochs on CPU, with validation carved out of the training data, and did not tune them.</p>

<h2>VII. Discussion and Limitations</h2>
<p class="ni">The measured effects are modest and specific. Hinglish pre-training lowers the Hinglish error without a significant English cost, which suggests that part of the English to Hinglish gap in MuRIL comes from its pre-training data and not from the task. Because HingRoBERTa also has a different tokenizer and 17% more parameters, we cannot separate pre-training data from those factors, and an XLM-R control would help. The remaining gap of about 0.09 and the within-source gap of about 0.1 mean that data quality, label noise, and the English content of the "hindi" tweets still account for part of what looks like a language effect.</p>
<p>Stress detection did not benefit from any change we tried except isolating it from the other tasks, and even that changed ranking but not F1, and made calibration worse. The stress label is a crowd judgment of English Reddit text, so none of these results transfer to another language, platform, or population without new labeled data. We did not evaluate stress on Hindi or Hinglish and the application flags those outputs as unvalidated.</p>
<p>The study has further limits. We ran three seeds on a CPU with a three-epoch budget, and for several runs the best validation epoch was the last, so the models may be under-trained; no learning-rate search was run. The bootstrap intervals cover test-sample noise only. The Hinglish data is small, the YouTube set has no documented annotator agreement, and one validation subset is unreliable. The contamination scan used exact matches on a re-upload of the corpus. Spelling normalization, transliteration {{ cite('roark','madhani') }}, and a human-annotated error taxonomy remain untested. Training times recorded for resumed runs count only the final process.</p>

<h2>VIII. Conclusion</h2>
<p class="ni">Under a controlled, multi-seed protocol on public data, a Hinglish-pre-trained encoder narrowed the English to Hinglish sentiment gap by about a quarter, sharing one encoder across unrelated tasks gave no benefit, and longer stress inputs did not help. Abstention and a faithfulness check give practical limits on when the outputs can be trusted: abstaining raises macro-F1 on the answered inputs, and the token attributions should not be trusted for Hinglish text. The next steps are a longer emotion training run with a stronger encoder, a GPU run with five seeds and a learning-rate search, a human-annotated Hinglish error taxonomy, and a test of transliteration and spelling normalization. We release the configurations, frozen splits, per-run outputs, and analysis scripts so that these experiments can be repeated.</p>

<h2>Ethics and Data Availability</h2>
<p class="ni">This work is a research screening study of language signals. It makes no diagnosis, and its probabilities are statistical model outputs. The datasets are public Reddit, Twitter, and YouTube text of a sensitive kind, so the repository holds no raw text: it contains frozen split identifiers with text hashes, configurations, per-example probabilities, and code. Identifiers in the text are masked before modeling, and the application neither logs nor stores submitted text. Crisis information in the application links to two international helpline directories and contains no telephone numbers. Source licenses are summarized in the repository and should be checked before any redistribution.</p>

